@pierretasci this is the `Dockerfile` docs, *which have little to do with how the image is run*. 
In addition, `CMD` in `Dockerfile` is `args` in the `ContainerSpec`. `ENTRYPOINT` in the `Dockerfile` is `command` in the `ContainerSpec`. If you specify `command` in the `ContainerSpec` it completely overrides the image `ENTRYPOINT`, and pass it directly (no shell, nothing) — If you specify `args` on the other hands (without `command`), it passes those as arguments to the image `ENTRYPOINT`, which can be anything, no guarantee still that there is a shell.
From a `ContainerSpec`, the only way you can assume there is a shell available is if you know the image has one *and* you run it explicitely.

