This is something we should address as part of this PR. With We have multiple options to solve this:

Option 1: We delete the logic to resolve parameters in the `displayName` as part of the [apply.go](https://github.com/tektoncd/pipeline/blob/main/pkg/reconciler/pipelinerun/resources/apply.go#L308) for matrixed tasks and only rely on the resolution happening as part of the `childReferences`. This will also make sure that the `status.pipelineSpec` does not have a resolved `displayName` as it will be inaccurate:

```
$ k get pr pr-echo-5c7wg -o json | jq .status.pipelineSpec
{
  "tasks": [
    {
      "displayName": "Message is Hello World!",
      "matrix": {
        "params": [
          {
            "name": "HELLO",
            "value": [
              "hi",
              "hey"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "name": "echo-hello",
      "taskRef": {
        "kind": "Task",
        "name": "scan-task"
      }
    }
  ]
}

```

Option 2: We fallback on the `matrix` prefix in the `displayName`. Such references will only be resolved in the `childReferences`. **Pro:** cleaner and easy to implement. **Con** introducing a new way of referencing params which can be avoided by option 1.

Struct fields no, interfaces yes.

The backwards compatible way to make this change would be to break this into separate interfaces, and have the consuming implementations satisfy both.

e.g.


```go
type Request interface {
	Name() string
	Namespace() string
	Params() pipelinev1.Params
}

type URLRequest interface {
	URL() string
}

...

type myRequestImpl struct {
	Request
	URLRequest
}
```

Or alternatively if you wanted to do an either-or approach:

```go
type NamespacedRequest interface {
	Name() string
	Namespace() string
}

type Request interface {
	NamespacedRequest
	Params() pipelinev1.Params
}

type URLRequest interface {
	NamespacedRequest
	URL() string
}
```

I don't think it matters too much which direction, but can you consolidate the v1 and v2 implementations to the same logic that are exposed with different functions?

e.g.

```go
// package v1

type Resolver struct {
  impl v2.Resolver
}

func (r *Resolver) Resolve(ctx context.Context, params []v1.Param) (framework.ResolvedResource, error) {
  return r.impl.Resolve(ctx, *v1beta1.ResolutionRequestSpec{Params: params})
}
```

We should avoid having duplicate implementations of the same thing if possible.

Worth calling out that the compatibility concern for the original Resolver interface ends here, since the actual implementation of the reconciler is unexported.

You could use this as a means for backwards compatibilty into v1 here by simplifying the problem as instead of going from v1 to v2, going from 2 different resolver types in the same version. This might solve some of the "v2 having a v1 API" issues we're seeing elsewhere.

e.g. something like:

```go
func NewController(ctx context.Context, resolver Resolver, modifiers ...ReconcilerModifier) func(context.Context, configmap.Watcher) *controller.Impl {
...
}

func NewRequestController(ctx context.Context, resolver RequestResolver, modifiers ...ReconcilerModifier) func(context.Context, configmap.Watcher) *controller.Impl {
...
}
```

I think this should be doable, since you should always be able to wrap this in a backwards compatible way:

```go
type compatResolver interface {
  old Resolver
}

func (*compatResolver) ValidateRequest(ctx context.Context, req *v1beta1.ResolutionRequestSpec) error {
  // if any field but params set, error
}

func (r *compatResolver) ResolveRequest(ctx context.Context, req *v1beta1.ResolutionRequestSpec) (ResolvedResource, error) {
  return r.Resolve(req.Params)
}
